SPECS.newbuild = { name:"New Build", region:"England",
 cats:["Planning","General Notes","Site Preparation & Contaminants","Structure","Foundations","Ground Floors","External Walls","Separating Walls","Internal Walls","Intermediate Floors","Roofs","Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs","Staircases & Guarding","Means of Escape & Fire Regs","Sound Insulation (Part E)","Windows, Doors & Glazing","Ventilation","Overheating (Part O)","Condensation (C2)","Drainage","Water & Sanitary (Part G)","Services","Access (Part M)","Security (Part Q)","EV Charging & Broadband (Parts S & R)","Conservation of Fuel & Power","Extra Notes & External Works","Renewable Energy"],
 buildups:[
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations", t:"Trench Fill Foundation", u:"Wall + 300 wide, 1000 deep",
   tgt:"To Approved Document A Section 2E and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) — width from Table 10 for the load and subsoil, or the structural engineer's design",
   p:[
    "Mass concrete trench fill foundation not less than 750mm thick, of a width not less than the thickness of the wall plus 300mm and in no case less than 450mm, taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground level and cast against undisturbed ground in a trench excavated to the full width. Concrete to BS EN 206:2013 (+A2:2021) and BS 8500-2.",
    "Foundations supporting internal loadbearing walls to be taken to not less than 600mm below finished ground level. Foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and are not to undermine it. Sulfate-resisting cement, or the BS 8500 design chemical class given by the ground investigation, to be used where the ground or the groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the ground falls the foundation is to be stepped. No step is to exceed the thickness of the foundation, and the higher foundation is to overlap the lower by not less than twice the height of the step, the thickness of the foundation, or 300mm, whichever is the greater — not less than 1m where the foundation is trench fill. Masonry below ground to be stepped to suit.",
    "All work in accordance with Requirement A1/2 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) Code of Practice for Foundations. The depth and the size of the foundation are to be approved on site by the Building Control Officer to suit the conditions found in the excavation.",
    "NOTE — The width is to be confirmed against Table 10 of Approved Document A for the wall load and the subsoil, or against the structural engineer's design. Where the wall is thin, the wall thickness plus 300mm can fall below the 450mm minimum for trench fill; the 450mm governs.",
    "NOTE — Where adverse soil conditions, a change of soil type, made ground, soft spots or major tree roots are found in the excavation, work is to stop, building control is to be notified and the structural engineer's advice obtained before any concrete is placed."
   ]},
  {g:"FD", c:"Foundations", t:"Strip Foundation", u:"Wall + 300 × 225, 1000 deep",
   tgt:"To Approved Document A Table 10 and Section 2E — thickness not less than the projection each side and not less than 225mm",
   p:[
    "Concrete strip foundation with the width taken from Table 10 of Approved Document A for the wall load and the subsoil, not less than the thickness of the wall plus 300mm, and the thickness of the concrete not less than the projection of the foundation beyond each face of the wall and in no case less than 225mm. Concrete to BS EN 206:2013 (+A2:2021) and BS 8500-2, cast on undisturbed ground and taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground level.",
    "Foundations supporting internal loadbearing walls to be taken to not less than 600mm below finished ground level. Foundations are to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and are not to undermine it. Sulfate-resisting cement, or the BS 8500 design chemical class given by the ground investigation, to be used where the ground or the groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the ground falls the foundation is to be stepped. No step is to exceed the thickness of the foundation, and the higher foundation is to overlap the lower by not less than twice the height of the step, the thickness of the foundation, or 300mm, whichever is the greater — not less than 1m where the foundation is trench fill. Masonry below ground to be stepped to suit.",
    "All work in accordance with Requirement A1/2 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended) and BS 8004:2015 (+A1:2020) Code of Practice for Foundations. The depth and the size of the foundation are to be approved on site by the Building Control Officer to suit the conditions found in the excavation.",
    "NOTE — The width is to be confirmed against Table 10 for the wall load and the subsoil, or against the structural engineer's design.",
    "NOTE — Where adverse soil conditions, a change of soil type, made ground, soft spots or major tree roots are found in the excavation, work is to stop, building control is to be notified and the structural engineer's advice obtained before any concrete is placed."
   ]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Full Fill Cavity Wall", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K, being the notional dwelling value in Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 1.1, with the final target set by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "External cavity wall of 103mm facing brick outer leaf, a 100mm cavity fully filled with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K106 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) leaving a nominal residual gap only, and a 100mm aircrete inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK thermal conductivity (Celcon Solar, Thermalite Turbo or equivalent, 2.9N with the strength grade confirmed by the structural engineer), finished internally with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs with a continuous ribbon of adhesive at the perimeter of every board, around openings and at services.",
    "Boards to be fitted with vertical joints staggered, tightly butted and free of mortar snots, and the cavity kept clean with a cavity board lifted as the work proceeds. Stainless steel wall ties to BS EN 845-1 at 450mm vertical and 900mm horizontal centres, staggered, at not more than 300mm vertical centres within 225mm of jambs, reveals, unbonded edges and movement joints (PD 6697), set with a fall to the outer leaf.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for mortar joints, wall ties and an air-gap correction of 0.01. With a 0.15 W/mK block it calculates at 0.16; with 115mm K106 in a 115mm cavity at 0.14.",
    "NOTE — The SAP calculation for the dwelling sets the U-values actually required. The notional dwelling has walls at 0.18, floors at 0.13, roofs at 0.11, windows at 1.2 and doors at 1.0 W/m²K with an air permeability of 5 m³/(h·m²); a dwelling built to these values with the notional services will meet the target emission, primary energy and fabric energy efficiency rates. Any relaxation of one element must be compensated elsewhere and confirmed in the design-stage SAP before construction."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Partial Fill Cavity Wall", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Where a clear residual cavity is required for exposure, external cavity wall of 103mm facing brick, a 150mm cavity with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K108 Cavity Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fixed against the inner leaf with the retaining discs on the ties and a 50mm clear residual cavity maintained, and a 100mm aircrete inner leaf of 0.11 W/mK with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs.",
    "Boards to be butted tightly, with the joints taped where the manufacturer requires, and the cavity kept free of mortar droppings. Wall ties to be the type and length specified for a 150mm cavity in BS EN 845-1 and the tie manufacturer's data, at the centres in EW1.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for mortar joints, ties and an air-gap correction of 0.01."]},
  {g:"EW", c:"External Walls", t:"Timber Frame External Wall", u:"0.16 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.18 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Timber frame external wall of 103mm facing brick outer leaf tied to the frame with stainless steel flexible ties at 600mm horizontal and 375mm vertical centres, a 50mm clear drained and vented cavity, breather membrane, 9mm OSB3 sheathing, 140mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres to the frame designer's design with the full stud depth filled with 140mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 Framing Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK), a continuous vapour control layer, and 37.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard with joints taped. Where a 25mm service void is wanted for the cabling it is to be formed on the room side of the insulated board, not in place of it.",
    "Cavity barriers of mineral wool in a polythene sleeve or proprietary barriers at every floor level, at the head of the wall, around all openings and at the junction with separating walls, and the sole plate on a damp proof course not less than 150mm above external ground level. Breather membrane laps not less than 100mm, and weep holes and cavity trays as for masonry cavity walls.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.16 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 by the combined method, allowing for the studs bridging the insulation at 9.5% of the area at 400mm centres and an air-gap correction of 0.01. The insulated plasterboard is what carries it: with 12.5mm plasterboard and a service void alone the same wall calculates at 0.20. Mineral wool of 0.035 W/mK between the studs calculates at 0.27 and is not to be used here."]},
  {g:"SW", c:"Separating Walls", t:"Separating Wall Between Dwellings — Cavity Masonry", u:"45 dB",
   tgt:"To achieve airborne sound insulation of not less than 45 dB DnT,w + Ctr, 60 minutes fire resistance and a party wall U-value of zero for SAP",
   p:[
    "Separating wall between attached dwellings to comprise two leaves of 100mm aircrete or dense aggregate blockwork of the density required by the chosen Robust Detail or by the pre-completion test design, with a 75mm minimum cavity, wall ties restricted to type A butterfly ties at not more than 2.5 per square metre, a 8mm parge coat or render to the cavity face where the detail requires it, and each leaf finished with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs with a continuous adhesive ribbon at the perimeter.",
    "The cavity is to be fully filled with mineral wool of not less than 10 kg/m³ and effectively sealed at the top with a flexible cavity closer and at the ends where it meets the external wall, so that the wall qualifies for a U-value of zero in the SAP calculation as a fully filled and edge-sealed party wall. Where the cavity is left open or unfilled, SAP assigns 0.2 W/m²K to the wall and the fabric target may not be met.",
    "The wall is to be carried up tight to the underside of the roof covering with the junction fire stopped with mineral wool, and no combustible material other than roof battens and underlay is to cross the wall in the roof void. Flues, sockets and recesses are not to be chased into the wall back to back. Where the wall is built to a Robust Detail, the detail is to be registered with Robust Details Ltd before work begins and the plot number confirmed on the checklist submitted to building control; otherwise pre-completion testing on a sample of the dwellings is required.",
    "NOTE — Impact sound insulation is not a requirement for the separating walls of houses. The 45 dB standard is 2 dB more onerous than for a conversion; do not use the conversion detail on a new build."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Walls", t:"Internal Stud Partition", u:"40 dB Rw",
   tgt:"To achieve a laboratory sound reduction of not less than 40 dB Rw between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and other rooms (Approved Document E, Requirement E2)",
   p:[
    "Internal partition of 89mm x 38mm C16 studs at 400mm centres with head and sole plates and solid noggins at 1200mm centres and at board edges, the stud depth filled with 50mm minimum mineral wool of 10 kg/m³ or more, and 12.5mm plasterboard of not less than 10 kg/m² each side, joints staggered, taped and filled, with all perimeters sealed. This is one of the constructions in Approved Document E Section 5 that meets Requirement E2 without testing.",
    "Where a partition carries a floor or roof load it is to be to the structural engineer's design and doubled or increased in section as required. Provide additional studs or plywood pattresses for the fixing of sanitaryware, radiators, kitchen units and stair handrails."]},
  {g:"IW", c:"Internal Walls", t:"Internal Masonry Wall", u:"40 dB Rw",
   tgt:"To meet Requirement E2 between a bedroom or WC and other rooms",
   p:[
    "Internal loadbearing wall of 100mm aircrete or dense blockwork to the structural engineer's design, in 1:1:6 mortar, finished with 12.5mm plasterboard on dabs or 13mm plaster. A 100mm block wall with a minimum mass of 120 kg/m² including finishes meets Requirement E2 without testing; where lightweight aircrete of lower mass is used the wall is to be plastered both sides or lined with plasterboard on dabs of 10 kg/m² minimum."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Beam and Block Ground Floor — Insulation Over", u:"0.12 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.13 W/m²K, being the notional dwelling value, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Precast prestressed concrete beams and 100mm infill blocks to the manufacturer's design, grouted and brushed with a sand and cement slurry, a polyethylene damp proof membrane of not less than 1200 gauge lapped and taped and linked to the damp proof course, 130mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) with 25mm perimeter upstand insulation, a 500 gauge separating layer, and a 75mm sand and cement screed with underfloor heating pipes where specified.",
    "A minimum 150mm clear ventilated void beneath the beams, with telescopic ventilators through the external walls giving not less than 1500mm² per metre run of wall or 500mm² per square metre of floor, whichever is the greater, on at least two opposite sides and continuous around the building. The oversite is to be cleared of vegetable matter and covered with 100mm concrete on hardcore, or 50mm concrete on a damp proof membrane, and covered with 50mm of concrete on a damp proof membrane where ground gas is a risk.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.12 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a floor with a perimeter/area ratio of 0.5 on clay, typical of a detached house of 8m x 8m; a semi-detached house with a ratio of 0.6 calculates at 0.13 with 130mm and at 0.11 with 150mm. The perimeter/area ratio for the actual footprint is to be entered in the calculation before the thickness is confirmed."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Ground-Bearing Slab — Insulation Over Slab", u:"0.12 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.13 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Excavate to formation removing all vegetable soil, 150mm minimum well compacted hardcore of clean broken stone blinded with 50mm sand, 1200 gauge polyethylene damp proof membrane lapped and taped and turned up to link with the damp proof course, 100mm minimum GEN1 or RC25/30 concrete slab reinforced with A142 mesh where the engineer requires it, 130mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard with 25mm perimeter upstands, a separating layer, and a 75mm screed.",
    "A ground-bearing slab is only to be used where the site investigation confirms the ground is not shrinkable clay with trees within influencing distance, not made ground, and the fill depth does not exceed 600mm; otherwise the floor is to be suspended as GF1. This build-up calculates at 0.12 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a perimeter/area ratio of 0.6 on clay."]},
  {g:"GF", c:"Ground Floors", t:"Suspended Timber Ground Floor — Insulation Between Joists", u:"0.13 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.13 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Suspended timber ground floor of 47mm x 220mm C24 joists at 400mm centres to the engineer's design on treated wall plates and sleeper walls with a damp proof course beneath every bearing, 200mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 Floorboard (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fitted tightly between the joists on treated battens or breathable netting with the joints taped, a breathable membrane over the joists lapped up the walls behind the skirting, and 22mm tongued and grooved P5 chipboard glued at the joints and screwed to the joists.",
    "A minimum 150mm clear void beneath the joists over an oversite of 100mm concrete on compacted hardcore or 50mm concrete on a 1200 gauge damp proof membrane, with the oversite surface not lower than the external ground, cross-ventilated by telescopic ventilators through the external walls at not less than 1500mm² per metre run or 500mm² per square metre of floor, whichever is the greater, on opposite sides. This build-up calculates at 0.13 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 13370 for a perimeter/area ratio of 0.5 on clay allowing for the joists and the ventilated void; 150mm calculates at 0.17 and meets only the limiting value.",
    "NOTE — A suspended timber ground floor is the hardest of the three floor types to get to 0.13 because the ventilated void draws heat from beneath; where the SAP relies on the notional value, the beam and block or ground-bearing options need less insulation for the same result."]},
  {g:"IF", c:"Intermediate Floors", t:"Intermediate Floor — Timber Joists or Metal Web Joists", u:"40 dB Rw",
   tgt:"To meet Requirement E2 between a bedroom or WC and other rooms, and 30 minutes fire resistance (REI 30) as a floor in a two-storey house",
   p:[
    "Intermediate floor of 47mm x 220mm C24 solid joists or engineered metal web joists at 400mm or 600mm centres to the designer's specification, with 22mm tongued and grooved P5 chipboard glued at all joints and screwed to the joists, 100mm mineral wool of not less than 10 kg/m³ laid between the joists, and a 12.5mm plasterboard ceiling of not less than 10 kg/m², or 15mm where the floor requires 30 minutes fire resistance with a ceiling of a single layer.",
    "Herringbone or solid strutting at mid-span for spans over 2.5m and two rows for spans over 4.5m, joists doubled under partitions running parallel and trimmed around stairwells and flues to the engineer's design, and joist hangers to BS EN 845-1 where joists bear on masonry. Where the floor is in a three-storey house the fire resistance is 30 minutes for the floor and the stair enclosure is to be a protected stairway."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation at Ceiling Level (Cold Roof)", u:"0.11 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.11 W/m²K, being the notional dwelling value, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Trussed rafter roof to BS EN 14250 and the truss designer's design, with tiles or slates on treated softwood battens over a vapour permeable underlay to BS 5534, diagonal and longitudinal bracing to the truss designer's requirements and the wall plate strapped down at not more than 2m centres with 30mm x 5mm galvanised straps 1m long. Ceiling of 12.5mm plasterboard with a vapour control layer, and 100mm mineral wool quilt of 0.044 W/mK between the ceiling ties with a further 300mm laid cross-wise over, to a total of 400mm.",
    "Ventilation equivalent to a continuous 10mm gap at the eaves on two opposite sides with 25mm proprietary eaves ventilators and insulation stops to keep the ventilation path clear, plus ridge or high-level ventilation equivalent to a 5mm continuous gap where the underlay is not vapour permeable. The loft hatch to be insulated to the same standard and draught sealed. Water tanks and pipes in the roof void to be insulated on all faces other than the underside over the ceiling insulation.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.11 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 allowing for the joist fraction at 400mm centres; 300mm total calculates at 0.15 and does not meet the notional value."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation Between and Under Rafters (Room in Roof)", u:"0.11 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.11 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Cut roof or attic trusses of 47mm x 200mm minimum rafters at 400mm centres to the engineer's design, tiles or slates on battens and counter-battens over a vapour permeable underlay to BS 5534, 200mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fully filling the rafter depth, and 72.5mm Kingspan Kooltherm K118 insulated plasterboard fixed through to the rafters with joints staggered and taped.",
    "With the rafter depth fully filled the underlay must be vapour permeable and the batten space above ventilated by the counter-battens; where a non-permeable underlay is used a 50mm ventilated gap above the insulation is required and the rafter depth increased. A continuous vapour control layer on the warm side sealed at all laps, junctions and penetrations.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.11 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres, allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Pitched Roof — Insulation Between and Over Rafters (Warm Roof, Sarking Board)", u:"0.12 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.11 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Where the rafters are to be exposed internally or the room-in-roof is to have a flat ceiling line without an under-rafter board, insulate over and between: 150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K107 Pitched Roof Board (thermal conductivity 0.019 W/mK) fully filling 47mm x 150mm rafters at 400mm centres, a further 75mm K107 laid over the rafters as a continuous sarking layer with the joints staggered, a vapour permeable underlay to BS 5534 over the sarking board, counter-battens fixed through the sarking into the rafters with the manufacturer's long fixings at the specified centres to carry the tile battens and the roof load, and 12.5mm plasterboard with a vapour control layer beneath the rafters or the rafters left exposed with a vapour control layer above them.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.12 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 at 400mm rafter centres allowing for rafter bridging and an air-gap correction of 0.01; 100mm over the rafters calculates at 0.10. The counter-batten fixings are structural: the tile battens, the roof covering and the wind uplift are carried through the sarking board into the rafters and the fixing schedule is to be from the board manufacturer for the pitch and exposure. Eaves, verge and ridge details are to allow for the raised roof line, and the wall plate strapping is unaffected.",
    "NOTE — This form of roof raises the roof surface by the thickness of the over-rafter board plus the counter-battens; on a semi-detached or terraced house the ridge and verge will not line through with the neighbour and the planning drawings must show it."]},
  {g:"RF", c:"Roofs", t:"Warm Deck Flat Roof", u:"0.10 W/m²K",
   tgt:"To achieve a maximum U-value of 0.11 W/m²K, confirmed by the SAP assessment",
   p:[
    "Warm deck flat roof of 47mm x 200mm C24 joists at 400mm centres to the engineer's design, 18mm exterior grade plywood or OSB3 decking laid to finished falls of not less than 1:40 on firrings, a vapour control layer fully bonded to the deck with all laps sealed, 200mm Kingspan Thermaroof TR26 LPC/FM (thermal conductivity 0.022 W/mK) in two layers break-bonded and fully adhered, and a single ply or high performance built-up felt waterproofing system to the manufacturer's specification and BBA certificate with 150mm upstands, edge trims and outlets.",
    "This build-up calculates at 0.10 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946 fully adhered. Where a green or terrace roof is specified the joists and deck are to be designed for the additional load and the waterproofing root-resistant.",
    "NOTE — Flat roofs on new dwellings are commonly used for dormers and single-storey elements only; where a flat roof is a large proportion of the roof area the SAP assessor is to be told at design stage because the notional dwelling value of 0.11 applies to it."]}
 ],
 notes:[
  {c:"Planning", t:"Planning Permission and Conditions", p:[
    "The dwelling is to be built strictly in accordance with the approved planning drawings and the conditions of the permission. Pre-commencement conditions are to be discharged in writing before work starts, and conditions relating to materials, levels, landscaping, drainage, ecology and construction management are to be checked against this specification. Where a condition requires a standard that exceeds the Building Regulations, for example optional requirement M4(2) or the water efficiency standard of 110 litres per person per day, the condition governs and this specification is to be read as amended.",
    "Building Regulations approval does not confer planning permission, and planning permission does not confer Building Regulations approval."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Compliance and Scope", p:[
    "All work to be carried out in accordance with the Building Regulations 2010 as amended, the current Approved Documents and the British Standards referred to in them, and to the drawings and the structural engineer's details. Where a British Standard has been superseded the current edition applies. Materials to be fit for their purpose, CE or UKCA marked where a designated standard applies, and installed in accordance with the manufacturer's instructions and any third-party certificate.",
    "Where there is a conflict between documents, the drawings take precedence for dimensions and the engineer's information for structure. Discrepancies are to be referred to the designer before the work proceeds. Nothing in this specification relieves the contractor of the obligation to build in accordance with the Regulations."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Statutory Notices to Building Control — Regulation 16", p:[
    "Regulation 16 of the Building Regulations 2010 (as amended in October 2023) requires three notices to the building control body: notice of intention to start work, given so that at least two clear days elapse before work begins; a commencement notice within five days of the work being regarded as commenced under Regulation 46A, which for a new dwelling is when the sub-structure including the ground floor structure is complete (for other work, when 15 per cent of the work is complete), after which the authority has four weeks to reject the notice; and a completion notice within five days of completion. The completion notice must give the name, address, telephone number and email of the client, the principal contractor and the principal designer, state that the work is complete, include a signed statement by the client that the work complies with the Building Regulations, and include signed statements from the principal designer and principal contractor confirming that they have fulfilled their duties under Part 2A with the dates of their appointments; where a statement cannot be obtained the reason is to be given.",
    "Inspection stages to be notified as they arise: excavations before concreting, foundations before covering, damp proof courses and membranes, oversite, drains before backfilling and on test, structural steel and fire-resisting construction before it is closed up, insulation before it is boarded, and completion. Certificates from competent person schemes (electrical, gas, oil and solid fuel appliances, unvented hot water) and the commissioning notices for fixed building services, ventilation and the air pressure test are to be given to building control within the periods the Regulations set (normally five days of the test or commissioning) and copied to the client with the completion certificate, which purchasers' solicitors will ask for.",
    "NOTE — The two-day and five-day limbs are separate duties. Missing the commencement notice means the work is not treated as commenced for transitional purposes, which matters where an edition of the Approved Documents is about to change (Approved Document L 2026)."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"CDM, Party Wall and HMOs", p:[
    "The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015 apply: where more than one contractor is involved the client is to appoint a principal designer and a principal contractor in writing, a construction phase plan is required before work starts, and the health and safety file is to be handed to the client at completion; where the client is a domestic client and makes no appointment the duties pass to the contractor or to the designer in control of the pre-construction phase.",
    "Where the new dwelling is attached to or built within 3m of a neighbouring building, or excavations fall within 6m and below the 45 degree line from the neighbour's foundations, notices under the Party Wall etc. Act 1996 are to be served (two months under Section 2 for work to a party wall, one month under Section 6 for adjacent excavation) with the engineer's drawings, and an award made where the adjoining owner dissents; no notifiable work is to start until the Act has been complied with. Where the dwelling is designed for occupation by three or more people forming more than one household sharing facilities it is a house in multiple occupation and the local housing authority's HMO standards and licensing apply alongside the Building Regulations, with a higher alarm grade, fire doors to all bedrooms and emergency lighting normally required; confirm the intended occupation with the client in writing."]},
  {c:"General Notes", t:"Information to be Provided to Building Control", p:[
    "Before work starts: structural calculations and details, the design-stage SAP calculation with the predicted energy rating, the site investigation report, the drainage design and the Robust Details registration or pre-completion test proposal. At completion: the as-built SAP and BREL compliance report with photographic evidence of the fabric and services, the air pressure test certificate, the sound test results or Robust Details completion certificate, the electrical, gas and heating commissioning certificates, the ventilation commissioning sheet, the water efficiency calculation, and the Energy Performance Certificate.",
    "The Home User Guide, the operating and maintenance instructions for the heating, hot water, ventilation and any renewable systems, and the fire safety information are to be handed to the occupier at completion."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation & Contaminants", t:"Demolition and Existing Services", p:[
    "Where an existing building or structure is demolished to make way for the dwelling, notice under Section 80 of the Building Act 1984 is to be given to the local authority not less than six weeks before demolition starts and its Section 81 notice complied with, the utilities disconnected and capped at the boundary by the suppliers, asbestos surveyed to the Control of Asbestos Regulations 2012 and removed by a licensed contractor before demolition, and existing drains, foundations and basements located, grubbed out or filled with compacted material and recorded on the drawings; old foundations are not to be built on unless the engineer has assessed them. Existing trees to be protected to BS 5837 where retained and the planning condition complied with."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation & Contaminants", t:"Site Preparation", p:[
    "Strip all vegetable soil and organic matter from the footprint and remove roots. Where the site has been previously developed, filled or is near a former industrial use, a desk study and intrusive site investigation to BS 10175 is to be carried out and any remediation strategy agreed with the local authority before work starts. Ground gas and radon protection as the following note.",
    "Subsoil drainage is to be provided where the water table is high or the site is on a slope with groundwater, and existing land drains intercepted and re-routed clear of the building."]},
  {c:"Site Preparation & Contaminants", t:"Radon and Ground Gas Protection", p:[
    "Where the site is in a radon affected area on the UK radon map, protection to BR 211 is to be built in. Basic protection is a radon-resistant membrane, of a grade certified for radon resistance (typically 1600 gauge / 400 micron polyethylene or a reinforced or aluminium-cored membrane), laid across the whole footprint in place of the standard damp proof membrane with laps of not less than 300mm sealed with gas-resistant tape, every penetration sealed with a top hat, and the membrane carried across the cavity to link with a cavity tray or the damp proof course in both leaves so the barrier is continuous; the slab is to be laid over sound compacted fill so the membrane cannot tear. Full protection, where the map shows the higher probability band, adds a radon sump beneath the slab connected to a capped pipe taken to the outside so a fan can be fitted later, or, for a suspended floor, a cross-ventilated void of not less than 150mm with the vents on opposite sides; a radon test after occupation and the fan fitted if the result is high. Where the site investigation identifies landfill gas, hydrocarbons or volatile organic compounds, gas protection is to be designed to BS 8485 and CIRIA C735 with a specialist membrane, its installation independently inspected and a verification report given to building control.",
    "NOTE — Confirm the radon status for the site postcode on the UK radon map before the ground floor build-up is issued; the membrane grade and whether a sump is needed depend on it."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Structural Design — General", p:[
    "All structural work, including foundations, masonry, steelwork, timber floors and roofs, lintels, padstones, restraint and the disproportionate collapse provisions, to be to the design and details of the structural engineer and to the engineer's satisfaction on site. Calculations and details to be submitted to building control before the relevant work begins.",
    "Masonry to BS EN 1996 and PD 6697, with movement joints, wall ties and lateral restraint as set out in the notes that follow and in the Additional Notes for Walls and Roofs. Wall ties, lintels, straps and hangers to BS EN 845 in stainless steel or the corrosion class specified."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Beams, Lintels and Fire Protection", p:[
    "Structural steel beams, posts and lintels to BS EN 10025 fabricated and erected to the engineer's design with bolted connections, bearing plates and padstones of the size and strength specified, bearing not less than 150mm unless otherwise detailed; steel to be primed and, where it forms part of a floor or the protected stairway enclosure, protected for 30 minutes (60 in a four-storey house or the separating wall) by encasement in fire-resisting board with staggered joints or by an intumescent coating applied to the certified thickness for the section factor, with the certificate given to building control. Lintels over openings in cavity walls to be proprietary insulated steel lintels to BS EN 845-2 with a thermal break, or separate lintels to each leaf, or precast prestressed concrete lintels, from the manufacturer's tables for the span and load with not less than 150mm end bearing and a cavity tray with stop ends above every external lintel; openings wider than 3m or carrying point loads to the engineer's design."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Lateral Restraint, Strapping and Openings", p:[
    "Floors and roofs are to provide lateral restraint to the walls: 30mm x 5mm galvanised mild steel straps not less than 1m long at not more than 2m centres at every floor level to walls parallel to the joists, fixed across three joists with noggins and packing, at the wall plate to the gable and flank walls with the straps turned down 100mm into the cavity or over the inner face, and to flat roofs in the same way with the wall plate held down against uplift. Trussed rafter roofs to have the wall plate strapped and the gable strapped to the rafters and ceiling ties with longitudinal and diagonal bracing to PD 6693-1.",
    "Openings in loadbearing masonry walls are to comply with Approved Document A Section 2C: the combined width of openings in a wall between buttressing walls or returns is not to exceed two thirds of the wall length, no opening is to be within 550mm of a return unless the return is designed as a pier, the return or buttressing wall at each end of a storey-height wall is to be not less than 550mm long, and the height of walls, the storey heights and the wall thicknesses are to be within the limits of the tables or to the engineer's design. Every wall is to be tied at its head to the floor or roof it supports."]},
  {c:"Structure", t:"Timber", p:[
    "Structural timber to be strength graded and marked to BS EN 14081, C16 or C24 as specified, and preservative treated where in contact with masonry or at risk of wetting. Trussed rafters to BS EN 14250 with bracing to PD 6693-1 and the truss designer's layout. Engineered joists and beams to the manufacturer's design with the specified hangers and web stiffeners. All timber to be protected from the weather on site and moisture content checked before boarding out."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Foundations", p:[
    "Foundations to the structural engineer's design following the site investigation. Where the ground is a firm shrinkable clay, strip or trench-fill foundations are to be not less than 1000mm deep and deeper where trees are within influencing distance in accordance with NHBC Chapter 4.2, with compressible material against the trench sides where heave is a risk. Strip foundations of GEN1 concrete not less than 600mm wide and 225mm thick, or trench fill not less than 450mm wide; concrete to BS 8500 with the designated sulfate class, or sulfate-resisting cement, where the ground or groundwater requires it.",
    "Where the structural engineer is not designing the foundations and the ground is a firm clay, sand or gravel without trees, fill or soft spots, Approved Document A Section 2E gives the fallback: strip foundations of GEN1 concrete not less than 225mm thick with the width from Table 10 for the wall load and soil type (not less than the wall thickness plus 300mm in most domestic cases, and never less than a projection each side equal to the thickness), taken to not less than 1000mm below finished ground in clay and 750mm elsewhere, with internal loadbearing walls on foundations not less than 600mm deep.",
    "Foundations to be stepped where the ground falls, with the step not exceeding the thickness of the foundation and the higher foundation overlapping the lower by not less than twice the step, the thickness or 300mm whichever is greater (1m for trench fill), and the masonry below ground stepped to suit. Foundations to be taken below the invert of any adjacent drain and not undermined by it; drains passing through foundations to be sleeved or bridged with a lintel with 50mm clearance all round and flexible joints each side. All excavations to be inspected by building control before concrete is placed, and soft spots dug out and filled with concrete."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Raft and Piled Foundations", p:[
    "Where the ground is of low or variable bearing capacity, is made ground, or is at risk of settlement, a reinforced concrete raft foundation to the engineer's design may be used in place of strips, with edge beams and thickenings under loadbearing walls, a compressible blinding and a damp proof membrane beneath, and the slab acting as the ground floor; the raft is to be designed to BS EN 1992 with the ground parameters from the investigation, the reinforcement lapped 450mm with 40mm cover and inspected before the pour. Piled foundations with ground beams where trees, fill or soft ground make shallow foundations impracticable, to the specialist's design with load tests where the engineer requires them."]},
  {c:"Foundations", t:"Substructure Walls and Damp Proof Course", p:[
    "Substructure walls below damp proof course in Class B engineering brick or dense concrete blockwork of 7.3N in 1:½:4 mortar, cavity filled with lean-mix concrete to not more than 225mm below the damp proof course and finished with a fall to the outside, weep holes at 900mm centres above the fill. Damp proof course of flexible bitumen polymer or pitch polymer to BS 6398 in both leaves at not less than 150mm above finished external ground level, lapped with the floor damp proof membrane and stepped where the ground falls.",
    "Airbricks to a suspended floor void to be sleeved through the cavity and set above the damp proof course. Where the external ground is above the internal floor level on any side, the wall is a retaining wall: see the Walls Below Ground and Tanking note."]},
  {c:"Ground Floors", t:"Ground Floor General Notes", p:[
    "Perimeter upstand insulation to all ground floors to reduce the thermal bridge at the wall junction, and the floor insulation carried to the inner face of the wall insulation without a gap. Screeds to be laid in bays of not more than 40m² with movement joints at doorways and expansion strips at all perimeters, and allowed to dry at one day per millimetre before floor finishes are laid. Where underfloor heating is installed the screed is to be a minimum 65mm over the pipes with the pipes clipped to the insulation and a polyethylene separating layer beneath.",
    "Service ducts and pipes passing through the floor to be sleeved and the sleeve sealed to the damp proof membrane and to the air barrier."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Movement Joints and Wall Ties", p:[
    "Vertical movement joints in the outer leaf at not more than 12m centres in clay brickwork, 7.5m to 9m in calcium silicate brickwork and dense concrete blockwork and 6m in aircrete blockwork, at not more than 1.5m from corners in parapets and at half the spacing in parapets and free-standing walls, and wherever the length of a panel exceeds three times its height or the wall changes thickness or height; joint width not less than 1.3mm per metre between joints in clay brickwork (16mm at 12m) and 10mm in other masonry, formed with a compressible filler and a low-modulus sealant and tied across with debonded ties. Wall ties to BS EN 845-1 of the type and length for the cavity width (PD 6697: 200mm long for a 50–75mm cavity, 225mm for 76–100mm, 250mm for 101–125mm, 275mm for 126–150mm), at 900mm horizontal and 450mm vertical centres staggered (2.5 per m²), at not more than 300mm vertical centres within 225mm of jambs, unbonded edges and movement joints, set level or with a slight fall to the outer leaf and kept free of mortar droppings; ties for cavities over 150mm to the manufacturer's data."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Damp Proof Courses, Cavity Trays, Closers and Weep Holes", p:[
    "Horizontal damp proof courses as the Substructure Walls note, with vertical damp proof courses at every reveal and abutment where the cavity is closed. Cavity trays with 150mm upstands, stop ends and not fewer than two weep holes per tray at not more than 900mm centres over every opening and external lintel, at abutments with lower roofs (stepped where a pitched roof abuts the wall), at the eaves of lean-to roofs, at the head of the wall below copings and parapets, and at the base of the cavity where it does not extend 225mm below the damp proof course. Insulated proprietary cavity closers to all door and window jambs, sills and heads, achieving a minimum path resistance of 0.45 m²K/W and closing the cavity for fire where required, with the frames set to the line of the closer so the insulation line is continuous. Vapour control layers to insulated framed walls of not less than 500 gauge polyethylene or a taped foil facing, lapped 100mm onto the floor membrane and sealed."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Walls Below Ground and Tanking", p:[
    "Where the finished ground level is above the internal floor level on any side, the wall below ground is a retaining wall to the engineer's design and is to be waterproofed to BS 8102:2022 to the grade for the use of the room behind: for a habitable room Grade 3, normally a Type A barrier membrane or tanking slurry to the external face carried up to the damp proof course with a protection board and a land drain at the base connected to the surface water system, combined with a Type C cavity drainage membrane internally where the water table or the consequence of leakage requires two forms of protection; for a garage or store Grade 1 or 2. The damp proof course is to be stepped and linked to the tanking, with the cavity fill and weep holes as the Substructure Walls note.",
    "NOTE — Where the dwelling has a full basement, the Basement specification build-ups and BS 8102 notes are to be used in place of this note."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Solid Walls and Timber Frame Cladding Alternatives", p:[
    "A solid masonry external wall of 215mm brick or 100mm rendered dense block cannot achieve the notional value on its own and is to be lined internally with 100mm Kingspan Kooltherm K112 between 100mm x 50mm studs set 25mm clear of the wall with 37.5mm insulated plasterboard, which calculates at 0.17 W/m²K to BS EN ISO 6946, with a condensation risk analysis to BS EN ISO 13788 because the masonry is kept cold; it is not the preferred construction for a new dwelling and is to be used only where planning requires a solid-wall appearance that cannot be achieved with a cavity wall. Where a partial fill or full fill cavity wall needs more margin, insulated plasterboard on dabs internally may be added and the U-value recalculated.",
    "The timber frame wall may carry a render on carrier board, tile hanging or timber or fibre cement cladding in place of the brick outer leaf, on treated battens and counter-battens over the breather membrane with a drained and ventilated cavity of not less than 25mm, insect mesh at the head and base, cavity barriers at every floor level and around openings, and the cladding fire-classified as Approved Document B requires for its distance from the boundary; the U-value is not materially changed by the cladding and the 140mm K112 frame calculates at 0.14 W/m²K with every finish. External wall insulation to a new dwelling is only appropriate on a solid wall and is to be a complete certificated system on a masonry substrate."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Low Pitched, Hipped and Cold Flat Roofs", p:[
    "Pitched roofs are to be laid at not less than the minimum pitch for the covering in the manufacturer's data and BS 5534 (typically 22.5 degrees for interlocking concrete tiles, 30 degrees for plain tiles and 25 degrees for slates, with some low-pitch tiles rated to 12.5 degrees); below the minimum pitch the roof is to be treated as a flat roof with a fully supported membrane. Hipped roofs to have the hip rafters and the jack rafters to the engineer's or truss designer's design with the hip irons and hip tiles mechanically fixed and the wall plate strapped at the corners. Cold deck flat roofs, with the insulation between the joists and a ventilated void above, are not to be used on a new dwelling; every flat roof is to be a warm deck as the Roofs build-ups."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Roof Coverings, Verges, Eaves, Leadwork and Valleys", p:[
    "Roof coverings fixed to BS 5534 with every tile fixed at the perimeter, ridge, hip and verge, and the fixing schedule for the site exposure obtained from the tile manufacturer. Dry-fix ridge and verge systems or bedded verges with an undercloak, and the underlay carried into the gutter over an eaves support tray. Fascia, soffit and bargeboards in treated softwood or cellular PVC, with the soffit ventilated as the roof build-up requires.",
    "Leadwork to the Rolled Lead Sheet Manual: Code 4 for soakers, cover flashings and small aprons, Code 5 for valley gutters, back gutters and dormer roofs, Code 6 for wide gutters, with piece lengths limited to 1.5m, laps and upstands as the manual and patination oil applied on completion; stepped and cover flashings tucked 25mm into the masonry joint and wedged. Lead or GRP flashings at all abutments with not less than 150mm upstand, and stepped flashings to a cavity tray where the roof abuts a cavity wall. Valleys as lead-lined or GRP valleys on valley boards with a 100mm lap between lengths, or a proprietary dry valley where the pitch and tile allow. Vapour control and roof void ventilation as the Condensation note and the roof build-ups."]},
  {c:"Additional Notes for Walls & Roofs", t:"Rainwater Goods", p:[
    "Gutters and downpipes in PVC-U, aluminium or cast iron to match the planning approval, sized to BS EN 12056-3 for the roof area, laid to falls with outlets at the low points and downpipes connected to the surface water drainage through trapped gullies or back inlet gullies. Rainwater is not to discharge onto adjoining land or into the foul drain."]},
  {c:"Staircases & Guarding", t:"Staircases", p:[
    "Private stairs to Approved Document K: pitch not more than 42 degrees, rise between 150mm and 220mm, going not less than 220mm, twice the rise plus the going between 550mm and 700mm, all rises and goings in a flight equal, headroom of 2m over the pitch line and landings, and a handrail on at least one side at 900mm to 1000mm above the pitch line, on both sides where the flight is 1m or wider, with not less than 600mm clear between handrails where two are fitted. Tapered treads to have a going of not less than 50mm at the narrow end and to satisfy the going rule at the centre line (or 270mm from the narrow end where the stair is wider than 1m); landings at the top and bottom of every flight at least as long as the flight is wide and level, with no door swinging over a landing other than a cupboard door which leaves 400mm clear; open risers not to pass a 100mm sphere; timber stairs to BS 585 with the strings, treads and risers glued and wedged and the newels bolted to the floor structure. Where the planning permission imposes M4(2) the stair is to have a clear width of 900mm and be capable of taking a future stairlift (Access note)."]},
  {c:"Staircases & Guarding", t:"Guarding", p:[
    "Guarding to stairs, landings, galleries, balconies, roof terraces and any floor edge with a drop of more than 600mm, not less than 900mm high internally and 1100mm at external balconies, roof terraces and the edges of external steps or landings. Guarding to be designed for the horizontal loads in BS EN 1991-1-1 (0.36 kN/m for a dwelling), not climbable by children and not passing a 100mm sphere: a metal or timber balustrade with balusters at not more than 100mm clear, or a glass balustrade of laminated or toughened laminated glass to BS 6180 with a handrail unless the glass is a tested free-standing system, with the fixings to the manufacturer's structural calculation; the balcony structure, its drainage and the fire performance of its materials near a boundary as the drawings.",
    "Windows with a sill lower than 800mm above floor level are to be fitted with guarding to 800mm or restrictors limiting the opening to 100mm, and any window opening onto a roof used as a terrace to be guarded at 1100mm; where a window is relied on for night-time cooling under Part O the additional provisions in the Overheating note apply."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Smoke and Heat Alarms", p:[
    "Fire detection and alarm system to BS 5839-6, Grade D1 Category LD2, which exceeds the Approved Document B minimum of Grade D2 Category LD3 and follows the BS 5839-6 recommendation for new dwellings: mains powered smoke alarms with tamper-proof standby supply, interlinked, in the circulation space on every storey within 7.5m of every habitable room door and within 3m of every bedroom door, and in the principal habitable room, with a heat alarm in the kitchen. Where the kitchen is open plan to the stair or a habitable room, a heat alarm is required in the kitchen area and a smoke alarm in the adjoining area. Alarms to be ceiling mounted not less than 300mm from walls and light fittings, on a dedicated circuit or a circuit that also serves lighting so that a failure is noticed, and interlinked by cable or radio.",
    "A carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 is to be fitted in every room containing a fixed combustion appliance (Services note). A design, installation and commissioning certificate for the fire detection and alarm system to be given to building control on completion."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Large Dwellinghouses", p:[
    "Where any storey of the dwelling has a floor area exceeding 200m², the dwelling is a large dwellinghouse for BS 5839-6 and the fire detection system is to be at least Grade A Category LD2 for a house of three or more storeys or Grade D1 Category LD2 for a two-storey house, with a control and indicating panel where Grade A applies, and the escape distances within the storey are to be checked so that no point is more than 7.5m from a smoke alarm. Where the ground floor exceeds 200m² or the plan is deep, an additional escape route or a second protected stair may be needed and the strategy is to be agreed with building control before the layout is fixed."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Means of Escape from Two-Storey Houses", p:[
    "Every habitable room in the upper storey, other than a kitchen, to have an emergency egress window or external door with an unobstructed openable area of not less than 0.33m² and not less than 450mm in both height and width, the bottom of the openable area between 800mm and 1100mm above floor level, positioned so the occupant can be reached by a ladder from the ground. Ground-storey habitable rooms to open onto a hall leading to a final exit or to have an egress window or door. No inner room is to be a bedroom or a habitable room unless it has its own egress window, and the access room is not to be a kitchen."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Means of Escape from Three-Storey Houses", p:[
    "Where the dwelling has a floor more than 4.5m above ground level, the stair is to be a protected stairway enclosed in 30 minutes fire-resisting construction with FD30S doorsets (E 30 Sa, intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with self-closers to every habitable room and the kitchen opening onto it (Approved Document B does not require self-closers in a dwellinghouse; they are adopted as the house standard), leading to a final exit at ground level or to two alternative escape routes at ground level separated from each other by fire-resisting construction. Alternatively, the top storey is to be separated by fire-resisting construction with its own alternative escape route, or a sprinkler system to BS 9251 provided throughout the dwelling by a certificated installer with the water supply confirmed.",
    "Cooking facilities are not to be located in the protected stairway. Glazing in the enclosure to be fire-resisting to the same standard. Where the ground floor is open plan, the arrangement is to be agreed with building control before the plans are finalised, as an open-plan ground floor removes the protected route."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Fire Resistance, Cavity Barriers and Garage Separation", p:[
    "Floors in a two-storey house to have 30 minutes fire resistance (REI 30) and the loadbearing walls supporting them R 30; in a three-storey house the floors remain REI 30 and the elements supporting them R 30 (60 minutes throughout a house of four or more storeys), and the separating wall between attached dwellings is REI 60 as a compartment wall. Cavity barriers at the junction of every cavity with a separating wall or compartment wall, around openings, at the top of external wall cavities, at the eaves and verges where the roof void is not fire stopped, and so that no cavity exceeds the dimensions in Approved Document B Volume 1 Table 5.1.",
    "Integral or attached garages to be separated from the dwelling by 30 minutes fire-resisting construction (REI 30) to the wall and to any floor over, with any communicating door an FD30S doorset (E 30 Sa, intumescent strips and cold smoke seals) fitted with a self-closer, and the garage floor 100mm below the dwelling floor at the door or laid to fall to the garage door so that fuel spillage cannot run into the house. External fire spread, boundary distances and roof coverings are in the following notes."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"External Fire Spread — Boundaries, Unprotected Areas and Roof Coverings", p:[
    "External walls within 1m of the relevant boundary are to have 30 minutes fire resistance from both sides (60 minutes where they separate buildings), an external surface of Class B-s3, d2 or better, and unprotected areas (windows, doors and combustible cladding) limited to small areas not exceeding 1m² and not less than 4m apart. Walls more than 1m from the boundary may have unprotected areas up to the limits in Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 11 for a residential building: 5.6m² at 1m, 12m² at 2m, 18m² at 3m, 24m² at 4m, 30m² at 5m and unlimited at 6m or more, measured for each side of the building facing that boundary; where cladding is worse than Class B-s3, d2 and more than 1mm thick, half its area counts as unprotected. The relevant boundary is the site boundary or the centre line of an adjoining road, canal or river.",
    "Roof coverings within 6m of the boundary are to be classified BROOF(t4) to BS EN 13501-5, which tiles, slates, felt on a substrate, single-ply membranes, GRP systems and glass with the appropriate classification achieve; thermoplastic rooflights are limited in area and distance as Section 12 and are not to be within 6m of the boundary unless classified. The classification of the roof covering, the rooflights and any cladding is to be stated on the drawings, and the position of the relevant boundary shown on the site plan so building control can check the unprotected area calculation."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Access for the Fire Service (B5)", p:[
    "A fire and rescue service pumping appliance is to be able to reach within 45m of every point inside the dwelling, measured along the route a hose would take, from a road or a private drive of not less than 3.7m width between kerbs (3.1m at gateways), with a turning facility where a dead-end route exceeds 20m and the surface capable of carrying 12.5 tonnes; where the house is set back beyond 45m the drive is to be designed as an access route or the dwelling fitted with a sprinkler system to BS 9251 as an alternative agreed with building control. A fire hydrant is to be within 90m of the entrance where the water undertaker's mains allow, or an alternative supply agreed. The access route and hydrant position are to be shown on the site plan."]},
  {c:"Means of Escape & Fire Regs", t:"Internal Linings", p:[
    "Wall and ceiling linings to be Class C-s3, d2 or better in rooms and Class B-s3, d2 or better in circulation spaces, with small rooms of not more than 4m² permitted Class D-s3, d2. Plasterboard, plaster and painted masonry satisfy this; timber cladding and wall panelling in circulation spaces to be treated or limited in area to the lesser of half the floor area or 20m². Thermoplastic rooflights and light diffusers limited as Approved Document B Volume 1 Section 6."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation (Part E)", t:"Separating Walls, Pre-Completion Testing and Robust Details", p:[
    "Separating walls between attached dwellings to achieve not less than 45 dB DnT,w + Ctr airborne sound insulation in accordance with Approved Document E Table 1a for new build. Compliance to be demonstrated either by construction to a registered Robust Detail or by pre-completion testing, and the route chosen stated with the full plans.",
    "Where Robust Details are used, the plot registration and purchase statement with the unique reference number are to be given to building control before the separating elements are built, the details built exactly as the handbook including the flanking constructions, and the compliance certificate and a report of where each detail was used given to building control at completion; where the detail is not followed on site the plots revert to pre-completion testing.",
    "Pre-completion sound tests are to be carried out when the separating elements, doors and windows are complete but before carpets and soft floor coverings are laid, by a tester accredited by UKAS or registered under the Association of Noise Consultants scheme, at the rate of one set of tests for every ten dwellings of each construction type (a set being two airborne wall tests, and two airborne and two impact floor tests where separating floors exist), with the results submitted to building control before completion; a failed test is to be followed by remedial work agreed with the tester and a retest of the failed element and any others the building control body requires.",
    "Flanking construction is to be as the detail: the inner leaf of the external wall is to be of the mass or the construction the detail requires, the external wall cavity is to be stopped at the separating wall with a flexible closer, floor joists are not to be built into the separating wall unless the detail allows it, and services are not to be chased into the separating wall.",
    "NOTE — A detached house has no separating wall and Requirement E1 does not apply to it; Requirement E2 for internal walls and floors still applies, and the internal partition and intermediate floor build-ups are written to meet it without testing."]},
  {c:"Sound Insulation (Part E)", t:"Internal Walls and Floors (Requirement E2)", p:[
    "Internal walls between a bedroom or a room containing a WC and any other room, and internal floors, to achieve a laboratory sound reduction of not less than 40 dB Rw, using the constructions in Approved Document E Section 5 or a construction with a test certificate. The requirement does not apply to a wall containing a door, to a wall between an en-suite and its bedroom, or to the floor of a room over a garage, but the specified constructions are to be used throughout for consistency. No testing is required for internal walls and floors."]},
  {c:"Windows, Doors & Glazing", t:"Windows and External Doors", p:[
    "Windows to have a whole-window U-value not exceeding 1.2 W/m²K to match the notional dwelling, and in no case worse than the limiting value of 1.6 W/m²K, with the g-value and frame factor entered in SAP as manufactured; external doors 1.0 W/m²K notional and 1.6 limiting. Frames to be draught stripped, fitted with trickle ventilators where the ventilation strategy requires them, and installed with the frame sealed to the cavity closer internally with an airtightness tape and externally with a low-modulus sealant. Sills to have a projecting drip and a stooled end, and the frame to be set to the line of the cavity closer so the insulation line is continuous.",
    "Glazing in critical locations, being any glazing within 800mm of floor level in a wall, within 1500mm of floor level in a door or within 300mm of a door edge, to be safety glass to BS EN 12600 Class 2 or better and permanently marked. Windows within 800mm of floor level to be restricted or guarded as the guarding note.",
    "Rooflights to have a U-value not exceeding 1.7 W/m²K to match the notional dwelling and in no case worse than the limiting value of 2.2 W/m²K, assessed in the horizontal position as Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 1.1 requires (SAP 10 Appendix R corrects for the installed pitch) and on the developed area of the rooflight including any kerb supplied with it, with a proprietary insulated kerb of not less than 150mm to a flat roof and the flashing kit for the tile on a pitched roof; a rooflight in a room in the roof that is the only openable window is to give the purge ventilation area and, where relied on for escape, the egress dimensions."]},
  {c:"Ventilation", t:"Ventilation — Approved Document F", p:[
    "Ventilation to Approved Document F Volume 1 by one of the systems in Table 1.2: natural ventilation with background ventilators and intermittent extract fans (System 1), continuous mechanical extract (System 3) or continuous mechanical supply and extract with heat recovery (System 4). Where the design air permeability is less than 5 m³/(h·m²) at 50 Pa, or the dwelling is single-aspect, or a room has no external wall, System 1 is not to be used and System 3 or 4 is required, and the choice is to match the SAP input.",
    "Whole-dwelling ventilation rate not less than the greater of 0.3 litres per second per square metre of internal floor area and the rate for the number of bedrooms in Table 1.3: 19 litres per second for one bedroom, 25 for two, 31 for three, 37 for four and 43 for five, with an additional 6 for each further bedroom. Extract rates in Table 1.1: kitchen 30 litres per second intermittent from a cooker hood over the hob (fitted 650mm to 750mm above it, to BS EN 13141-3) or 60 elsewhere in the kitchen, or 13 continuous; utility room 30 intermittent or 8 continuous; bathroom 15 intermittent or 8 continuous; sanitary accommodation 6 intermittent or 6 continuous. Intermittent fans to BS EN 13141-4 may be wired to the light switch and are to have a 15 minute overrun where the room has no openable window.",
    "Background ventilators for System 1 to give an equivalent area of not less than 8000mm² in every habitable room and kitchen of a dwelling with more than one storey, 10,000mm² in a single-storey dwelling, and 4000mm² in every wet room (Table 1.7), with not fewer than four ventilators in a one-bedroom dwelling and five otherwise, set not less than 1700mm above floor level (trickle ventilators in the window heads ordered as part of the window units), the equivalent area marked or recorded, and noise-attenuating ventilators used on facades exposed to traffic or other noise. Purge ventilation to every habitable room by an openable area of not less than one twentieth of the floor area where the window opens more than 30 degrees, or one tenth where it opens between 15 and 30 degrees, or a mechanical system giving four air changes per hour; the openable areas are to be checked against the window schedule and the Part O free areas before issue. Internal doors undercut 10mm above the floor finish (7600mm² for a 760mm door).",
    "Continuous mechanical extract (System 3) to have 4000mm² background ventilators to each habitable room and not fewer than the number of bedrooms plus two; mechanical ventilation with heat recovery (System 4) to have no background ventilators, supply terminals placed to avoid draughts and wet-room air not recirculated; continuous rates not less than 13 litres per second from the kitchen and 8 from each bathroom and utility room and 6 from a WC, and the whole-dwelling rate as above. Ductwork rigid wherever possible with flexible duct limited to 1.5m at final connections, installed to BSRIA BG 43, insulated where it passes through unheated spaces and fire stopped or fitted with fire dampers where it passes through fire-resisting construction; all ducts to discharge to outside air through a terminal with a back-draught shutter. Every fixed mechanical ventilation system to be commissioned with the flow rates measured to BS EN 13141-6 or 13141-7 and recorded on the Approved Document F commissioning sheet, given to building control within five days of the final test and to the occupier in the Home User Guide."]},
  {c:"Overheating (Part O)", t:"Limiting Overheating Risk — Approved Document O", p:[
    "The dwelling is to be shown to comply with Requirement O1 by the simplified method in Approved Document O Section 1 or by dynamic thermal modelling to CIBSE TM59 in Section 2. Under the simplified method the location is first identified as moderate or high risk (high risk being the parts of Greater London and central Manchester defined in the Approved Document) and the dwelling as cross-ventilated or not; the glazing area in each orientation, for the dwelling and for the most glazed room, is then limited to the values in Tables 1.1 and 1.2, and the free area of openings for removing excess heat is to be not less than the minimum in Tables 1.3 and 1.4 for the risk category and ventilation type, with a separate minimum free area for every bedroom. Where the glazing exceeds the permitted area the dwelling fails the simplified method and modelling is required.",
    "Glazing in a high-risk location is to have external shutters with means of ventilation, or glass with a g-value of not more than 0.4 and a light transmittance of not less than 0.7, or an overhang giving a 50 degree altitude cut-off on due south facing facades (Approved Document O paragraph 1.9). External shading, a low g-value glass and the omission of large west-facing glazed areas are the fabric measures with the greatest effect in any location.",
    "Openings relied on for removing excess heat must be usable at night: windows that open more than 100mm and whose bottom of openable area is below 1100mm are to have guarding to Approved Document O Table 3.1 or restrictors, handles within 650mm reach of the inside face of the wall, grilles and security screens not passing an 8mm gap, and looped blind cords fitted with safety devices; noise, pollution and security are to be shown not to prevent the windows being opened at night, and where the site is on a busy road or the bedrooms face an unsecured area mechanical cooling or a modelled solution is likely to be needed. The compliance report and the Appendix B checklist are to be submitted with the full plans, the built dwelling is to match them, and the occupier is to be given the information on how the dwelling is kept cool in the Home User Guide.",
    "NOTE — Part O is assessed room by room for the worst-case bedroom. A large south or west facing bedroom window with no shading is the usual reason a house fails, and the fix is far cheaper at planning stage than after the frames are ordered."]},
  {c:"Condensation (C2)", t:"Interstitial and Surface Condensation", p:[
    "Vapour control layers on the warm side of all insulated timber constructions with laps sealed and penetrations taped; breather membranes to BS 4016 or BS EN 13859 on the cold side; roof voids ventilated or the roof constructed as a warm roof with a vapour permeable underlay. Junctions to be designed to the accredited construction details or the detail entered in SAP so that the temperature factor at every junction is not less than 0.75. Where a construction is not covered by an accredited detail, a condensation risk analysis to BS EN ISO 13788 is to be provided."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Foul Drainage — Below Ground", p:[
    "Below-ground foul drainage in 100mm PVC-U to BS EN 1401 or vitrified clay to BS EN 295, laid at not less than 1:40, or 1:80 where at least one WC is connected and the flow is adequate, bedded on and surrounded by 100mm of pea gravel with flexible joints, with not less than 600mm cover under gardens and 900mm under drives and roads (1200mm for rigid pipes under roads), or protected by a reinforced concrete slab on a compressible layer with 75mm of granular material between the pipe and the slab where the cover is less. Inspection chambers at every change of direction or gradient, at junctions and at not more than 45m centres, 450mm diameter to 1.2m deep, with double-sealed bolt-down covers inside the building and vehicle-rated covers in drives; rodding eyes at the head of each run.",
    "Drains passing through a wall or foundation to have rocker pipes not more than 600mm long each side and 50mm clearance in the opening, or the foundation carried down below the drain and the drain sleeved. Drains under the building to be kept to a minimum length with access at both ends, laid in a granular bed and surround, given a 1m wide strip of A142 mesh in the slab over the run and a 150mm concrete surround where the crown is within 300mm of the underside of the slab, and tested before the floor is placed over them. Building over or within 3m of a public sewer, over a manhole, or where the sewer is more than 3m deep or larger than 225mm, requires a build-over agreement with the sewerage undertaker before work starts, with the sewer surveyed by CCTV before and after and the foundations designed to bridge or clear it as the undertaker requires; manholes are not to be enclosed.",
    "Connection to the public sewer with the consent of the sewerage undertaker under Section 106 of the Water Industry Act 1991, or to off-mains treatment as the following note where no sewer is available. Where a WC or appliance is below the level of the sewer or gravity fall cannot be achieved, a packaged pumping station to BS EN 12056-4 and BS EN 12050 with a non-return valve, alarm and 24 hours' storage is to be provided in an accessible external chamber with the rising main discharging to a gravity manhole; a pumped WC or macerator is permitted only where a gravity WC is also available in the dwelling. All drains to be tested by air or water before backfilling and again at completion, witnessed by building control."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Above-Ground Sanitary Pipework", p:[
    "Above-ground pipework to BS EN 12056-2 with a 100mm soil and vent pipe terminating 900mm above any opening within 3m, 32mm branches to basins and 40mm to baths, showers and sinks, all with 75mm seal traps; branch lengths not exceeding 1.7m for a 32mm basin waste (3m at 40mm), 3m for a 40mm bath or shower waste (4m at 50mm) and 6m for a 100mm WC branch, beyond which anti-vacuum traps or a ventilated branch are needed. No branch is to connect within 200mm below a WC branch connection, the stack is to have a long-radius bend at its foot, and an air admittance valve to BS EN 12380 may replace an open vent only where at least one stack on the drain remains open to the air and the valve is above the highest spill-over level in an accessible ventilated position."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Septic Tanks, Treatment Plants and Drainage Fields", p:[
    "Where no public sewer is available within reasonable distance, foul drainage is to discharge to a packaged treatment plant to BS EN 12566-3 sized for the number of bedrooms plus two persons, with the effluent discharging to a drainage field designed to BS 6297 on a percolation test and sited not less than 15m from any building, 10m from a watercourse or permeable drain and 50m from a well, spring or borehole, or to a watercourse only with an environmental permit or under the general binding rules; new septic tanks discharging to a watercourse are not permitted. The installation is to be registered with the Environment Agency where required, sited within 30m of a vehicle hardstanding for desludging, and the maintenance schedule handed to the occupier. Cesspools are a last resort and require the local authority's agreement."]},
  {c:"Drainage", t:"Surface Water and SuDS", p:[
    "Surface water from roofs and paved areas to be disposed of, in order of preference, by infiltration to soakaways designed to BRE Digest 365 on a percolation test and located not less than 5m from any building and 2.5m from a boundary; to a watercourse with the consent of the riparian owner and the Environment Agency; or to a surface water sewer with the undertaker's consent. Permeable paving, rainwater harvesting and attenuation are to be used where the planning permission or the lead local flood authority requires them, and the design flow limited to the greenfield rate where a condition applies. No surface water is to be connected to the foul sewer."]},
  {c:"Water & Sanitary (Part G)", t:"Water Efficiency, Hot Water Safety and Sanitary Provision", p:[
    "The dwelling to be designed so the calculated water consumption does not exceed 125 litres per person per day using the water efficiency calculator in Approved Document G Appendix A, or 110 litres where the planning permission imposes the optional requirement, with the calculation submitted at completion. Fittings to be within the flow rates assumed: WCs of 6/4 litre dual flush or 4.5/3, basin taps at 5 litres per minute, showers at 8 litres per minute where 110 litres applies, and a bath capacity of not more than 185 litres to the overflow.",
    "Wholesome water to every kitchen sink and washbasin, and to the bath, shower and WC cistern; a WC with a washbasin with hot and cold water in the entrance storey or the principal storey where required by Part M. Hot water storage to Approved Document G3: the cylinder stored at not less than 60°C to control legionella, with a thermostat and a non-self-resetting energy cut-out so that the stored water cannot exceed 100°C; unvented systems to BS EN 12897 installed by a person registered with a competent person scheme for unvented hot water, with the temperature and pressure relief valve, expansion vessel, tundish within 500mm of the valve, D1 and D2 discharge pipework one size larger than the valve outlet routed to a safe visible termination and a warning label, and the work notified to building control. The hot water supplied to every bath limited to not more than 48°C by a thermostatic mixing valve to BS EN 1111 or BS EN 1287 fitted as close to the bath as practicable and set so that it cannot readily be altered by the user. Cylinder and primary pipework insulated to Approved Document L Volume 1 Table 4.4. Sanitary appliances and their connections to Approved Document G4 and G5, and all fittings and pipework to the Water Supply (Water Fittings) Regulations 1999."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Heating, Hot Water and Controls", p:[
    "Space heating and hot water to be as the SAP calculation, with a room-by-room heat loss calculation to BS EN 12831. New wet heating systems are to be designed for a maximum flow temperature of 55°C with emitters sized accordingly. Where a heat pump is specified, the system is to be designed to the CIBSE Domestic Heating Design Guide and MCS MIS 3005 with the emitter and cylinder sizing from the heat loss calculation, the outdoor unit located to the MCS 020 noise limit at the nearest neighbour's window, a hot water cylinder with an immersion for legionella control, and any back-up heater interlocked so that it never leads. Where a gas boiler is installed it is to be a condensing boiler of not less than 92 per cent ErP seasonal efficiency with a room-sealed flue, the terminal positioned to Approved Document J and the manufacturer's clearances and not less than 50mm from combustible material, inspection hatches of 300mm x 300mm at every joint and change of direction where a flue runs in a void, a carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 between 1m and 3m from the appliance and, for a combination boiler, one of the additional efficiency measures in the Domestic Building Services Compliance Guide (flue gas heat recovery, weather or load compensation, or a smart thermostat with automation and optimisation). Oil-fired appliances installed by an OFTEC registered technician with the oil tank not less than 1.8m from the building and 760mm from the boundary, on a fire-resisting base extending 300mm beyond the tank, bunded to 110 per cent where there is a pollution risk, and fitted with a fire valve.",
    "Controls to Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 5: a room thermostat or programmable thermostat with independent time and temperature control of each heating zone, two zones where the dwelling exceeds 150m², thermostatic radiator valves to all radiators other than in the room with the thermostat, a cylinder thermostat and time control for hot water independent of the heating, and a boiler or heat pump interlock so that neither runs when there is no demand; primary and accessible pipework insulated to Table 4.4. Electric storage heaters with automatic charge control and panel heaters with time and temperature control in each room; electric underfloor heating on a dedicated circuit with 30mA RCD protection and a floor sensor thermostat to each zone, with continuity and insulation tests recorded before the floor is covered.",
    "Wet underfloor heating designed to BS EN 1264 with the floor surface temperature limited to 29°C (27°C with resilient finishes), insulation beneath the pipes of not less than ten times the resistance of the floor finish (R 0.75 m²K/W minimum on intermediate floors), 25mm edge insulation, a 65mm minimum screed over the pipes with movement joints at doorways, zone boundaries and bays over 40m² or 8m long, the system pressure tested before the screed is placed and commissioned to BS EN 1264-4 with the manifold zones labelled. Hot water storage, unvented cylinders and bath temperature limitation as the Part G note. The system to be commissioned to the manufacturer's and CIBSE procedures, with the commissioning certificate, the Benchmark record and the heat loss calculation given to building control and the occupier.",
    "NOTE — Under the Future Homes Standard (Approved Document L Volume 1 2026 edition; dates in the Energy Performance Targets note) a new dwelling is expected to be heated without a fossil fuel boiler, with a heat pump and photovoltaics forming the notional specification. Where the application will be made after 24 March 2027, or where the client wants to avoid a later retrofit, design for the heat pump now: the emitter sizes, cylinder space and electrical supply are hard to add afterwards."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Electrical Installation", p:[
    "Electrical installation to BS 7671 and Approved Document P by a registered competent person who is to issue an Electrical Installation Certificate and notify the work to building control. Consumer unit with a metal enclosure and RCD protection to all circuits, arc fault detection where required, and surge protection. Sockets, switches and controls between 450mm and 1200mm above floor level with the consumer unit switches within the same band where Part M applies. All fixed light fittings to have an efficacy of not less than 75 light-source lumens per circuit-watt with independent switching to each room, and external lighting fitted with a daylight cut-off and controlled by a presence detector or a manual switch, as Approved Document L Volume 1 Section 6."]},
  {c:"Services", t:"Combustion Appliances, Chimneys, Hearths and Carbon Monoxide", p:[
    "Any solid fuel, gas or oil appliance to be installed to Approved Document J with the air supply, hearth, flue and chimney specified for the appliance; flues to be lined, terminals positioned clear of openings and boundaries to Approved Document J Diagram 3.4, and a notice plate fixed beside the meter or consumer unit recording the appliance the flue is suitable for. Open-flued appliances are not to be installed in bedrooms or bathrooms. Gas work by a Gas Safe registered engineer, oil by an OFTEC technician and solid fuel by a HETAS installer, or otherwise notified to building control, with the installation tested on completion and the commissioning certificates given to building control. A carbon monoxide alarm to BS EN 50291 in every room containing a fixed combustion appliance other than a cooker.",
    "Where a masonry chimney, flue or hearth is built: a chimney with a lined flue of the size for the appliance (150mm minimum for a closed appliance of up to 30kW, 200mm for an open fire), the liner to BS EN 1457 or BS EN 1856, the chimney terminating not less than 600mm above the ridge where within 600mm of it and otherwise outside the zones in Diagram 2.1, a constructional hearth of 125mm concrete extending 500mm in front of and 150mm each side of the opening or a superimposed hearth for a stove, combustible material kept 200mm from the flue and 40mm from the chimney, and a permanently open air supply of 550mm² for each kW of rated output over 5kW for a stove (for the whole rated output where the design air permeability is below 5 m³/(h·m²), which is usual in a new dwelling)."]},
  {c:"Access (Part M)", t:"Category M4(1) Visitable Dwelling", p:[
    "Unless the planning permission requires M4(2) or M4(3), the dwelling is to meet Requirement M4(1): a level or gently sloping approach from the parking space or the boundary to the principal entrance, or a ramped approach not steeper than 1:15 for flights up to 10m or 1:12 up to 5m with level landings, and steps only where the site gradient makes a ramp impracticable; an accessible threshold to the principal entrance with a clear opening width of not less than 775mm; a level landing of not less than 900mm x 900mm outside the door with a covered area where practicable.",
    "Within the entrance storey, corridors of not less than 900mm clear (750mm at a local obstruction not more than 300mm long; where a door opens off the corridor other than head-on, the corridor is 1200mm wide for a 750mm clear door, 1050mm for a 775mm door and 900mm for an 800mm door — Approved Document M Volume 1 Table 1.1), internal door clear openings of not less than 750mm, and a WC with a washbasin in the entrance storey or the principal storey with a clear space of 750mm in front of the pan and the door opening outwards. Switches and sockets at 450mm to 1200mm above floor level."]},
  {c:"Access (Part M)", t:"Category M4(2) Accessible and Adaptable Dwelling", p:[
    "Where the planning permission imposes optional requirement M4(2), in addition to M4(1): a step-free approach with a level or ramped route and a parking space capable of being widened to 3.3m; a principal entrance door with a minimum clear opening of 850mm and a 1200mm x 1200mm level landing; entrance storey WC with a clear 1100mm x 700mm zone, a basin that does not obstruct it and provision for future grab rails; a bathroom with a clear access zone to the bath or a level access shower and the wall reinforced for future adaptation; a living room and a bedroom on the entrance storey or a stair designed to accept a future stairlift with a 900mm clear width; kitchen and bedroom clear access zones; and the structure over the stair or a two-storey hall capable of taking a through-floor lift.",
    "Window sills in the principal living room at not more than 850mm above floor level to allow a seated view, and handles between 450mm and 1200mm. The full schedule in Approved Document M Volume 1 Section 2 is to be checked room by room against the drawings before submission."]},
  {c:"Access (Part M)", t:"Category M4(3) Wheelchair User Dwelling", p:[
    "Where the planning permission imposes optional requirement M4(3), either as a wheelchair adaptable dwelling (M4(3)(2)(a)) or, for a dwelling allocated by the local authority, a wheelchair accessible dwelling (M4(3)(2)(b)), the full schedule in Approved Document M Volume 1 Section 3 applies: a level approach with a parking space of 3.3m x 6m or capable of enlargement, a principal entrance door of 850mm clear with a 1500mm x 1500mm landing and a lobby of 1500mm x 1800mm where provided, halls of 1200mm with 1500mm turning circles, internal doors of 850mm clear, a wheelchair storage and transfer space of 1100mm x 1700mm near the entrance, general storage of 1.5m² for a one-bedroom dwelling, 2.0m² for two, 2.5m² for three and 3.0m² for four bedrooms, a through-floor lift or the space and structure for one on a dwelling of more than one storey, living rooms and kitchens with the clear zones and adjustable worktops, a principal bedroom with a 1500mm clear zone on both sides of the bed, a level access shower room on the entrance storey with the drainage and reinforcement for a future WC and basin layout, a bathroom with a bath and transfer space, handles between 700mm and 1000mm, and private outdoor space accessible from the living room at level threshold. The schedule is to be checked room by room against the drawings before submission and the furniture schedule shown on the plans."]},
  {c:"Security (Part Q)", t:"Secure Doors and Windows", p:[
    "All easily accessible doors and windows, being any on the ground floor, any on the basement level, and any above whose sill is within 2m of an accessible flat roof, balcony or climbable feature, to be secure doorsets and windows shown by test to meet PAS 24:2016 or an equivalent standard, with the certificate provided to building control. Doorsets to have the glazing laminated where within reach of the lock, a door chain or viewer, and multi-point locking; letter plates to have a flap and restricted aperture. Frames to be fixed to the structure with the fixings specified by the manufacturer's test."]},
  {c:"EV Charging & Broadband (Parts S & R)", t:"Electric Vehicle Charging", p:[
    "Where the dwelling has an associated parking space, an electric vehicle charge point is to be installed to Approved Document S: a minimum 7 kW untethered charge point with a Type 2 socket to BS EN 62196, a universal socket, smart functionality and a dedicated circuit with the supply capacity confirmed with the distribution network operator, positioned so a vehicle in the space can be charged. Where the parking is not within the plot, cable routes to the space are to be provided instead. Where the connection cost to the network exceeds £3,600 the exemption in the Approved Document may be claimed with evidence."]},
  {c:"EV Charging & Broadband (Parts S & R)", t:"Gigabit-Ready Broadband", p:[
    "In-building physical infrastructure to Approved Document R Volume 1: a duct of not less than 25mm from the network access point at the boundary to a termination point inside the dwelling, with a drawstring, and a gigabit-ready connection provided where the cost does not exceed £2,000 per dwelling, otherwise the next fastest connection available within that cost. The network access point and the internal termination point are to be shown on the drawings."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Energy Performance Targets", p:[
    "The dwelling is to comply with Approved Document L Volume 1 2021 by achieving a Dwelling Emission Rate, a Dwelling Primary Energy Rate and a Dwelling Fabric Energy Efficiency rate no worse than the target rates for the notional dwelling calculated by SAP 10.2, with each individual element no worse than the limiting values in Table 4.1: walls 0.26, floors 0.18, roofs 0.16, party walls 0.20, windows and doors 1.6, rooflights 2.2 W/m²K, and an air permeability of not more than 8 m³/(h·m²) at 50 Pa. A design-stage SAP and predicted EPC are to be submitted with the full plans, and the as-built SAP, BREL report and EPC at completion.",
    "Thermal bridging at every junction to be calculated using accredited construction details, manufacturer's calculated psi values or values from the SAP Appendix K table, and the junctions on site are to match the details used; the BREL photographic evidence is to record each junction type before it is covered. Construction is to be to the standard of workmanship in the Approved Document, with the insulation continuous and in contact with the air barrier.",
    "NOTE — Approved Document L Volume 1 2026 (the Future Homes Standard) comes into force on 24 March 2027 with a transitional period to 24 March 2028 for dwellings whose initial notice or full plans were given before that date and where work on that dwelling has commenced. Under the 2026 edition the fabric of the notional dwelling is broadly unchanged but the heating is by heat pump, photovoltaics are included, and the carbon target falls substantially. Confirm the edition that applies to the application before the SAP is run, and where the site will not commence before March 2028 design to the 2026 edition now."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"High-Efficiency Alternative Systems", p:[
    "Under Regulation 25A, before work starts the technical, environmental and economic feasibility of using high-efficiency alternative systems — decentralised energy from renewable sources, cogeneration, district or block heating and cooling, and heat pumps — is to be considered and documented, and the analysis made available to building control on request with a statement on the notice or deposit of plans that it has been carried out. The design-stage SAP with the heat pump and photovoltaic options compared normally satisfies this, and the record is to be kept on the job file."]},
  {c:"Conservation of Fuel & Power", t:"Airtightness and Pressure Testing", p:[
    "Every new dwelling is to be pressure tested at completion by a tester registered with a competent person scheme, and the measured air permeability entered in the as-built SAP. The design value is to be entered in the design SAP and achieved on site: a continuous air barrier is to be identified on the drawings (normally the plaster and plasterboard line internally and the vapour control layer in timber constructions), with all joints, penetrations and junctions sealed, plasterboard on dabs finished with a continuous perimeter ribbon, the sole plate, wall plate and floor zone sealed, window and door frames taped, and service penetrations grommeted. A dwelling designed with an air permeability below 5 requires a mechanical ventilation system (Ventilation note)."]},
  {c:"Extra Notes & External Works", t:"Refuse Storage and the Garage", p:[
    "Requirement H6 applies to a new dwelling: provide a hard level standing for the number and size of refuse and recycling containers the collection authority uses, within 30m of the dwelling and 25m of the collection vehicle position with a path not steeper than 1:12 and no steps, screened as the planning permission requires. Where an integral or attached garage is built, the separation from the dwelling is to be as the Fire Resistance note (REI 30, FD30S self-closing doorset and a 100mm step or fall), and the floor of any room over the garage is an exposed floor of the dwelling to be insulated to 0.13 W/m²K with 150mm Kingspan Kooltherm K103 between or over the joists and a 15mm fire-resisting board ceiling, which calculates at 0.12 to BS EN ISO 6946; the garage side of the separating wall is to be insulated as a wall to an unheated space."]},
  {c:"Renewable Energy", t:"Renewable and Low Carbon Technology", p:[
    "Where photovoltaic panels are specified in the SAP, the array is to be installed by an MCS-certificated installer with the orientation, pitch, peak output and inverter as the SAP input, and the roof structure checked for the additional load and wind uplift. Panels to be fixed with the manufacturer's roof hooks or in-roof system with the flashing and the underlay maintained, and an isolator and generation meter in an accessible location. Where a battery is installed it is to be to the manufacturer's instructions in a ventilated location with a fire-resisting enclosure where required. The commissioning certificates and MCS certificate to be given to building control and the occupier.",
    "Solar thermal collectors to be installed by an MCS installer with a twin-coil cylinder, an expansion vessel and a high-limit thermostat, and the pipework in the roof insulated with a high-temperature insulation. Air source heat pumps to MCS MIS 3005 sited to respect the MCS 020 noise limit of 42 dB(A) at the nearest neighbour's window, on a level base with a condensate drain, and not permitted development where within 1m of the boundary or on a listed building; ground source heat pumps with a horizontal collector at not less than 1.2m depth or vertical boreholes to the specialist's design and, where the ground loop is closed, no Environment Agency permit. Small wind turbines are rarely viable on a residential site and are not permitted development in most cases; waste water heat recovery on the shower drain to the SAP Appendix Q listing where the SAP relies on it. Every renewable system is to be entered in the SAP as installed, and the installer's MCS certificate is what the as-built SAP and the Smart Export Guarantee application require.",
    "Rainwater harvesting to BS EN 16941-1 with the storage sized for the roof area and the non-potable pipework labelled and separated from the wholesome supply by a fluid Category 5 backflow arrangement, and greywater recovery to BS 8525-1, may be specified where the SAP, the water efficiency calculation or the planning permission relies on them, with the maintenance regime handed to the occupier."]},
 ]};
